trajectory.patch:
An analysis of the `test_mutex_recursive` test case reveals a scheduling issue. The main test thread, which has a higher priority, creates a lower-priority waiter thread (`tThread_waiter`). After unlocking the mutex, the main thread calls `k_yield()`.

The `k_yield()` function only cedes the CPU to threads of equal or higher priority. Since `tThread_waiter` has a lower priority, it is never scheduled to run, and consequently, the `thread_ret` variable is never updated to `TC_PASS`. The subsequent assertion `zassert_true(thread_ret == TC_PASS, ...)` then fails as expected under these conditions.

To fix this, `k_yield()` should be replaced with a call to `k_sleep()`. This will suspend the main test thread, allowing the scheduler to run the now-ready, lower-priority `tThread_waiter`. The waiter thread will then acquire the mutex, set `thread_ret` to `TC_PASS`, and unlock the mutex. When the main test thread wakes up, the assertion will pass.

